WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.190
In a perfect world, movie lovers' pockets
would always be lined with enough cash to

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.420
buy tickets to every single film that ever
screened in theaters. Unfortunately, we live

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.420
in the decidedly flawed reality that makes
seeing and appreciating every movie pretty

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.610
much impossible.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:17.610
Year after year, a ton of incredible films
are released only to end up going largely

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.529
unnoticed, and 2018 is no different. Here
are some of the most underappreciated movies

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:24.860
you've already missed in 2018.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.240
I Kill Giants

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.039
Adapted from writer Joe Kelly and artist J.
M. Ken Niimura's graphic novel I Kill Giants,

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:35.570
Danish director Anders Walter's adaptation
stars Madison Wolfe as the plucky Barbara

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.940
Thorson. Barbara's an oddball outsider with
a bunny-ear cap, a head in the clouds, and

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.360
a Norse war hammer in her bag. You see, Barbara
fancies herself a killer of giants, and is

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.570
convinced that a horde of them are coming
to Earth and she's the only one who can vanquish

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.570
stop them.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.829
Blending together an impactful coming-of-age
tale, themes of grief and denial, gorgeous

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:57.360
magical realism, solid CGI, and captivating
performances from Wolfe and supporting actors

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.570
Imogen Poots, Zoe Saldana, and Sydney Wade,
I Kill Giants is a magnificent monster movie

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:07.259
that's monstrously moving. Critics have applauded
it as "never less than engrossing," and you'll

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:08.950
agree.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:09.950
Thoroughbreds

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.609
Murder is on the menu in writer-director Cory
Finley's Thoroughbreds, a black comedy thriller

00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:19.090
that centers around childhood friends Lily,
played by Anya Taylor-Joy, and Amanda, played

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.209
by Olivia Cooke, who reunite to cook up a
devious plan. Even though both are born and

00:01:23.209 --> 00:01:27.579
raised in the suburbs, Lily's years at boarding
school and an illustrious internship have

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:31.979
turned her into a prim and proper goody-two-shoes,
whereas Amanda's unidentified mental disorder

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:36.329
has turned her into an emotionless outcast
with a smart mouth and stinging wit.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.159
Opposites attract in this case, and the two
end up fueling the worst in one another as

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:44.759
they bond over a shared contempt: They both
hate Lily's tyrannical stepfather Mark, played

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:49.960
by Paul Sparks, and resolve to bring him to
a grisly end. Lily and Amanda's scheming leads

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.999
them to recruit drug dealer Tim, played by
the dearly departed Anton Yelchin, whom the

00:01:53.999 --> 00:01:58.020
girls manipulate into becoming their personal
hitman. The murder mission takes more than

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.880
a few unexpected turns, arriving at an end
you likely won't see coming.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.819
Rarely do first-time creatives get their debut
as right as Finley did with Thoroughbreds,

00:02:05.819 --> 00:02:10.560
a darkly alluring film. Most everyone agrees
that Thoroughbreds has the potential to be

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.989
a classic, and despite the film's lack of
big-budget promotion and pre-release hype,

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:17.750
it'll be revisited by audiences for years.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:18.750
The Endless

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.629
The Endless snagged a bunch of relentlessly
positive reviews after it opened in a limited

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:27.430
run on April 6, but was buried by its box
office competition, John Krasinski's A Quiet

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:32.200
Place, which debuted to $50 million that same
weekend. The Endless is a perception-twisting

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.709
thriller that takes one terrifying moment
brothers Justin and Aaron receiving a cryptic

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:41.129
video from members of a UFO death cult they
were once a part of and springboards into

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:42.130
the insane.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.879
Rather than burning the tape and getting on
with their lives, Justin and Aaron decide

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:50.180
to head back to the exact place they narrowly
escaped a decade earlier in hopes of finally

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.640
getting closure. As incomprehensible horrors
start surrounding the camp, Justin and Aaron

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.739
are forced to reconsider if the cult was actually
preaching the truth. A slow-burning horror

00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:03.359
that shocks in its second half but frightens
the whole way through, The Endless is an underappreciated

00:03:03.359 --> 00:03:07.950
movie you'll wish you had paid more attention
to before learning about it right now.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.219
Lean on Pete

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:13.459
Director Andrew Haigh's Lean on Pete perfectly
adapts Willy Vlautin's touching novel and

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:17.290
spreads it beautifully across the silver screen
for the world to see. It's a film festival

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.799
darling and a favorite among critics, but
has gone seriously underappreciated by casual

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:22.799
moviegoers.

00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:26.870
Lean on Pete follows burgeoning young star
Charlie Plummer as Charley, a fifteen-year-old

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.340
boy who moves to rural Portland with his father
Ray, played by Travis Fimmel. As his father

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.549
spirals further into himself, Charley discovers
companionship and camaraderie at a racetrack,

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:40.909
where he becomes the new attendant to a weathering
racehorse named Lean on Pete. While there,

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:45.689
he also befriends the horse's owner, Del Montgomery,
and his jockey Bonnie, played respectively

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:47.980
by Steve Buscemi and Chloë Sevigny.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.499
But don't let the premise fool you: Lean on
Pete is no light-hearted movie about a young

00:03:51.499 --> 00:03:55.790
boy and the love he feels for an aging horse
and his new pals. As critics have proclaimed,

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.940
the film is a "haunting tale of survival,"
a "heartbreaking look at a marginalized America"

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.219
that's "likely to leave you in tatters."

00:04:03.219 --> 00:04:04.219
Goldstone

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:08.099
Take everything you've learned about Australia
from Crocodile Dundee, internet jokes about

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:13.120
riding kangaroos to school, and the throw-a-shrimp-on-the-barbie
restaurant chain Outback Steakhouse and toss

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.389
it in the bin. Goldstone, from multi-talented
writer-director-composer-cinematographer Ivan

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:22.000
Sen, presents the Australian Outback in an
intimate, thrilling new way.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.550
Set in the titular town of Goldstone, the
movie follows Indigenous Detective Jay Swan,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.759
played by Aaron Pederson, as he journeys up
to the tiny mining outpost. He's there to

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:34.850
aid Josh, a young policeman played by Alex
Russell, and Goldstone's mayor, played by

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.540
Jacki Weaver, to investigate the disappearance
of a Chinese tourist. An already complex case

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.190
turns tense because of Jay's identity as both
an Aborigine and as a law enforcement officer.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.310
His presence in town ruffles the feathers
of both the racists of Goldstone and the town's

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:53.500
Aboriginal residents. When Jay discovers that
Josh and the Mayor may not be as innocent

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:58.240
as they'd like others to believe, the gripping
noir narrative takes yet another unexpected

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.240
turn.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.540
Released to high praise but underwhelming
box office in Australia in 2016, Goldstone

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.350
made its theatrical debut in the United States
in March of 2018, where it performed much

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.350
the same: while critics adored it, the raw,
sun-blasted pic didn't get as much attention

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.300
as it deserved.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.889
Gemini

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:19.039
The neo-noir thriller Gemini lives up to the
split personality implication of its title,

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:23.400
as you're never really sure of the true intentions
of its leading lady. The lady in question

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.680
is Lola Kirke as Jill LeBeau, the assistant
to Hollywood It girl Heather Anderson, portrayed

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.460
by Zoë Kravitz's. One evening, Jill discovers
Heather's lifeless body, the result of a gunshot

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.190
wound, slumped in pool of blood in her otherwise
immaculate multi-million dollar mansion, and

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:41.810
she decides to embark on a mission to solve
the mystery of her former boss' murder. Meanwhile,

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.530
Jill must also stay a step ahead of the relentlessly
determined Detective Edward Ahn, played by

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:51.690
John Cho, who senses she's the one who committed
the heinous crime. Can she? Or will the skeletons

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.800
Jill has long kept shoved in her closet finally
break free?

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.090
"Am I under arrest?"

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:57.139
"Not yet."

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:02.300
Written, directed, and edited by award-winning
independent filmmaker Aaron Katz, Gemini has

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.550
earned a mostly positive response with critics.
They've dubbed the kaleidoscopic film "a piece

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:10.849
of clean, confident visual storytelling" and
a "shimmering puzzler" that "warp[s] into

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:14.949
an unlikely detective story in the Cold Weather
vein." If that description doesn't entice

00:06:14.949 --> 00:06:19.629
you to catch this seriously underappreciated
movie, what will?

00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:20.629
Bomb City

00:06:20.629 --> 00:06:25.379
One part pulpy crime story, one part heart-grabbing
drama, Bomb City is a brilliant little film

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:29.389
that made a bang without the general public
even noticing. The Jameson Brooks-directed

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:35.009
film stars Dave Davis as Brian, a punk music-obsessed
teen from Amarillo, Texas who sticks out like

00:06:35.009 --> 00:06:38.970
a mohawked thumb in his conservative town.
Thankfully, there are a few other punks out

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.020
there who view Brian as a sort of mouthpiece
for the movement. Sadly, there are an equal

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.250
number of bullying jocks who regularly clash
with the punks. Tensions bubble below the

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.229
surface, then explode in a violent altercation
that comes with a fatal consequence.

00:06:51.229 --> 00:06:56.300
A native of Amarillo, director Brooks based
Bomb City on the real-life story of the town's

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.639
violent confrontation between a group of punks
and bullies. Not only does the film present

00:07:00.639 --> 00:07:05.129
the tragedy of the violence in a truthful
and harrowing light, it also serves as a reminder

00:07:05.129 --> 00:07:10.050
that the American justice system isn't always
moral. Not everyone is as unassuming or as

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.960
aggressive as their outward appearance may
suggest.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.210
Unsane

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.910
You may think you know director Steven Soderbergh,
but you've never seen him like this before.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.979
The mind behind films like Out of Sight, Erin
Brockovich, and Logan Lucky casts aside the

00:07:23.979 --> 00:07:28.990
traditional trappings of filmmaking and embraces
B-movie mojo with Unsane, a psychological

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:32.770
horror-thriller pic shot entirely on an iPhone
7 Plus.

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:37.470
The Crown actress Claire Foy leads the ambitious
film as Sawyer Valentini, a financial analyst

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:42.050
who moved away from her hometown in an attempt
to evade her longtime stalker, David Shrine,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:46.340
played by Joshua Leonard. A confession to
her therapist about her self-destructive thoughts

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:51.099
gets Sawyer a one-way ticket into a mental
institution, where she's held for 24 hours

00:07:51.099 --> 00:07:55.400
of observation. There, Sawyer is soon forced
to confront her darkest fear while trapped

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:00.710
inside the institution's walls. The only question
is: Is the fear real, or just a product of

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:01.849
her paranoia?

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:06.820
As disturbing as the experimental Unsane is,
it didn't drum up as much buzz as you'd expect.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:11.270
Still, though it's underappreciated by the
masses, it hasn't gone without critical praise.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.139
The New Yorker's Richard Brody calls Unsane
"one of [Soderbergh's] best movies … the

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:20.290
very spark of his artistic passion." All the
more reason to see it yourself.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:21.750
You Were Never Really Here

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.919
Joaquin Phoenix is in top form in You Were
Never Really Here, writer-director Lynne Ramsay's

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:30.780
film adaptation of Jonathan Ames' novel of
the same name. Phoenix gives a darkly intense

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:35.540
performance as Joe, a former law enforcement
agent and combat veteran struggling with post-traumatic

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:40.640
stress disorder. Joe lives out his post-FBI
days as a hired gun, earning cash to support

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.790
himself and his ailing mother, played by Judith
Roberts, by locating missing and trafficked

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:49.840
young girls. But he also experiences intense
suicidal fantasies, one of which almost becomes

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.560
reality before his boss, played by John Doman,
snaps him back to the surface world and tasks

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.240
him with finding 13-year-old Nina, the daughter
of a state senator.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.090
Though Joe is properly equipped for the violence
of the job, what he isn't prepared for is

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.210
the triple-layer twist and searing horrors
that await him.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:10.230
Touted as "stark, sinewy, [and] slashed-to-the-bone,"
You Were Never Really Here is a "masterclass

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:14.900
in filmmaking" more than worthy of the type
of global adulation its heavily-promoted cinematic

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:17.560
counterparts have received this year.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.900
Sweet Country

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:22.920
Another blistering drama out of Australia,
Sweet Country centers around Hamilton Morris

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.970
as middle-aged Aboriginal farmer Sam Kelly,
who works for a preacher in the Northern Territory.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:32.810
Sam and his family are sent to assist a cruel,
ill-tempered war veteran named Harry March,

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.740
played by Ewen Leslie, in renovating his cattle
yards in Alice Springs. As the two work together

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.870
to build the yards up, their relationship
quickly deteriorates, coming to a chaotic

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:46.920
head when Sam fatally shoots Harry in an act
of self-defense. As Sam flees across the unforgiving

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.320
Australian outback to outrun the relentless
Sergeant Fletcher and his hunting party, the

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.570
truth behind the killing comes out. In response,
the community is left to ponder whether capturing

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.050
Sam is justice or just vengeance.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.960
We suspect most skipped out on the limited
release of Sweet Country in favor of the widely

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:06.460
launched A Quiet Place, which made its massive
debut on the same day, and that's a shame.

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:10.310
Beyond its engrossing visuals and rhythmic
pacing, Sweet Country has captivated those

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:14.480
who did see it with its powerful and universal
story that lingers long after the credits

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:15.480
have rolled.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:16.980
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love, too!

